<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Melrose Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1136</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Melrose Abbey is a ruined Cistercian abbey in the Scottish Borders, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> I in 1136, the first Cistercian house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. It grew rich from sheep farming and the wool trade, and the heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a> is believed to be buried there. Repeatedly damaged by English armies, it was largely rebuilt in the late fourteenth century and declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/melrose-abbey.jpg" alt="Melrose Abbey" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gitta Zahn (@gittaz on Flickr) · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_of_St_Mary%27s_Abbey,_Melrose_(Scotland%27s_first_Cistercian_monastery,_1136),_showing_late_14th-century_Gothic_architecture_in_rose-colored_sandstone_with_medieval_cemetery,_burial_place_of_Robert_the_Bruce%27s_heart,_Scottish_Borders.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
